 { On occasion, a pawn advance can have the effect of clearing vital space for the king. Here are some examples on this topic.At first sight, Black seems to be on his way towards a safe draw by ...Kf7xf6, but there is a move that changes everything. } 1. { -KEY- } g4 $1 { Clearing a path for the king. } ( { a) The alternatives won't work; e.g. } 1. Kg3 $2 g5 ) 
 ( { b) Or } 1. Kf3 $2 Kf7 2. g4 Kxf6 ) 
hxg4 ( { Obviously, } 1... h4 { fails to } 2. g5 ) 
2. Kg3 Kf7 3. Kxg4 Kxf6 { From now on, White wins by means of a proper use of waiting moves. } 4. Kh4 $1 { As mentioned in the previous section, if possible waiting moves with the king are often preferable to ones with the pawns, which are better saved for later. } a5 ( { Obviously, } 4... a6 { would have been met by } 5. a3 ) 
5. a4 $1 { The idea of 'copying' the opponent's waiting moves is often useful on such occasions. } Ke7 ( { Or } 5... Kf7 6. Kg5 Kg7 7. h3 $1 Kf7 8. Kh6 Kf6 9. h4 { transposing - Baburin. } ) 
6. Kg5 Kf7 7. Kh6 $1 { King first! } ( { a) Neither } 7. h3 $2 Kg7 ) 
 ( { b) Nor } 7. h4 $2 Kg7 8. h5 gxh5 9. Kxh5 Kh7 { leads anywhere for White. } ) 
Kf6 8. h4 $1 { A final accuracy. } ( 8. h3 $2 Kf7 { just leads to a draw. } ) 
Kf7 9. Kh7 Kf6 10. Kg8 ( { In view of } 10. Kg8 g5 11. h5 g4 12. h6 g3 13. h7 g2 14. h8=Q { Black resigned. } ) 

