 { In a typical ending of the Spanish Exchange Variation, White has a pleasant edge, but how to progress? Ljubojevic found a nice solution. } 1. { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Clearing a path for the king towards g5. } hxg5 2. hxg5 fxg5 ( { Other moves, such as } 2... Nc6 $6 { would just lead to a problem with the f6-pawn after } 3. gxf6 gxf6 4. Ncd5 ) 
3. Kg4 Nc6 4. Kxg5 { As a result of the previous operation White now possesses a passed pawn on the e-file and a long-term target on g7. } Nd4 5. Rf2 Re8 6. Kf4 Re6 { Perhaps a bit bothered by the active enemy king, Gligoric tries to chase him away; unfortunately, the f6-square will never become available to the rook. } ( { a) Instead, } 6... Ne6 { is well met by } 7. Kg4 $1 { keeping the 'magic' distance. } ) 
 ( { b) But the simple } 6... c6 { depriving the enemy knights of the d5-square, seems like a better choice. } ) 
7. Ng4 Bh5 8. Nd5 $1 { Not just attacking the c7-pawn, also regrouping the knight to a more comfortable square on e3. } c6 9. Nde3 b4 $6 { This merely creates targets for White on the queenside. } ( { a) Instead, Black could have considered the pragmatic } 9... Re8 { preparing to meet } 10. c3 { by } Ne6 ) 
 ( { b) Or even } 9... Bxg4 $5 { getting rid of a piece that soon becomes a problem child. } ) 
10. c3 bxc3 11. bxc3 Nb5 12. c4 Nd4 13. Rb2 $1 { Interestingly, Ljubojevic had the possibility of Rh2 for several moves, but finally his rook finds a more useful working area. } Be8 14. Nf5 $1 { Swapping Black's most active piece. } ( { The tempting } 14. Rb8 $6 { promised less due to } Ne2 15. Kg5 Nd4 $1 ) 
Nxf5 15. Kxf5 Bd7 16. Ne5 $1 Bc8 17. f4 $1 { White is not scared by the discovered attacks, and just keeps improving his position. } ( 17. Rb8 { was also strong. } ) 
Ke7 18. Rb8 Rf6+ 19. Kg5 Kd8 20. f5 Kc7 21. Rb2 $1 { Preparing a shift of angle with Rh2-h7. } ( { Seeing that } 21. Rb2 Rh6 { is no real defence, in view of } 22. Ng4 Rh3 23. Rh2 { Black resigned. } ) 

