 { With a sounder pawn structure and more active pieces White is better, but Black defends for the moment. } 1. { -KEY- } h4 $1 { Gaining space and preparing to soften up the enemy kingside pawns. } Ng8 { Quite a passive choice. } ( { Perhaps Black should have tried } 1... h5 $5 { , preventing the further course of the game; e. g. } 2. Kf4 ( { not } 1... Ng8 1. Nf5 $2 d5 $1 3. cxd5 Nxf5 4. Kxf5 a6 { etc. } ) 
Ke6 3. Kg5 g6 { , followed by ...Kf7. White, on his part, could have eliminated this option by playing 1.g4!? on the first move instead. } ) 
2. g4 Nf6+ 3. Kf4 Ke6 ( { In the event of } 3... Kc6 { White should try something like } 4. Kf5 ( { and avoid } 3... Ke6 3. d5 $2 Kc5 ) 
Kd7 5. d5 { with similar play as in the game. } ) 
4. d5+ $1 Kf7 5. Nf5 { The d4-d5 thrust didn't just eliminate the ...d6-d5 break and fix a weakness on d6, it also opened a path for the knight via d4-c6 (b5). } Ne8 6. Nd4 Nc7 7. h5 $1 { Gaining more space. As with Larsen's 5.a4! in the previous example, when making such a move you can't predict its concrete benefits, you just know that it will favour you in some way later on. } Kf6 8. Nc6 a5 ( { On } 8... a6 { White would proceed just like in the game. } ) 
9. g5+ Kf7 10. Na7 $1 { In knight endings, the knight should usually move around a lot, as we discussed in Chapter Four. } Na6 $1 { Black follows the same recipe. } 11. Nc8 Nc5 12. Nxd6+ $1 { Activity above material. } ( { After } 12. Nxb6 $6 Ke7 { it is not clear how to progress with White. } ) 
Kf8 13. Ke5 Nxa4 14. Ne4 $1 { Betting on the passed pawn. } Nb2 ( { Or instead } 14... Ke7 15. d6 Kd8 16. Kd5 Nb2 17. c5 $1 bxc5 18. Nxc5 a4 19. Ke6 { and wins. } ) 
15. d6 $1 Nxc4+ ( { In the event of } 15... Nd3 { Moskalenko gives a nice variation: } 16. Ke6 Ke8 17. d7 Kd8 18. Kd6 a4 ( { or } 18... g6 19. Nf6 Nf4 20. h6 { etc. } ) 
19. g6 h6 20. Ng5 $1 { and wins. } ) 
16. Ke6 Ke8 17. Nf6+ $1 { A nice touch, one that neatly shows the potential benefits of pushing your pawns as far as possible in the endgame. } ( { Also possible was } 17. d7 Kd8 18. Nf6 $1 { in the same spirit, for example } a4 19. Nxh7 a3 20. h6 $1 { and the g-pawn decides the game. Even } 18. g6 { works, since after } hxg6 19. hxg6 Ne5 { -else Ng5-f7 decides- } 20. Kxe5 Kxd7 { there is again } 21. Nf6 $1 { when one of the pawns on b6 or g7 falls. In any case, the game continuation has the practical merit of being much easier to calculate. } ) 
Kd8 ( { If instead } 17... gxf6 { then } 18. d7 Kd8 19. g6 { etc. } ) 
18. Nxh7 Nxd6 19. Kxd6 Ke8 20. Ke6 a4 21. g6 $1 ( { In view of the continuation } 21. g6 a3 22. h6 a2 23. hxg7 a1=Q $11 24. g8=Q { Black resigned. } ) 

