 { White has some initiative, but how to make progress? } 1. { -KEY- } a4 $5 { Another abstract space-gainer, with echoes of Larsen. } ( 1. h4 $5 { is a worthy alternative in the spirit of this chapter, with the concrete point that after } Qe5 2. Nxe7 Qxe7 3. Qf5 $1 { , White installs the dual threat of 4.Qxh7 and 4.Rd7, as noticed by Qrtebdda. Perhaps Radjabov wasn't convinced by the rook endgame arising after } g6 4. Rd7 gxf5 5. Rxe7 fxe4 { , although after } 6. Rxe4 { followed by Kg2-f3, Black's kingside soon comes under a lot of pressure. } ) 
 ( 1. Nxe7 Qxe7 2. Qf5 { is a similar idea, but definitely it makes sense to include the useful h2-h4 in the process, if possible. } ) 
Nxd5 ( { Having seen the rest of the game, it is easy to suggest } 1... a5 { as a possible improvement. Perhaps Mamedyarov didn't like the look of } 2. Nc3 { heading for b5, although after } Nc6 3. Nb5 Rd8 { Black should be okay. } ) 
2. exd5 Rd8 ( { Now there is no time for } 2... a5 { because of } 3. d6 $1 ) 
3. a5 $1 b5 4. d6 $1 { Anyway! Radjabov has seen that the trade of the d5- and a6-pawns will yield him a dangerous passer on the a-file. } Rxd6 5. Rxd6 cxd6 6. Qc6 Qd8 7. Qxa6 b4 { Black appears to be holding his own, but Radjabov now launches the same idea on the opposite flank. } 8. h4 $1 { Whatever happens next, this advance is bound to create new opportunities. Besides, the h2-square might become useful for the king. } Qc7 ( { In the event of } 8... h5 $5 { White could continue } 9. c3 { just like in the game, with the hope that the h5-pawn becomes exposed in some future variation. } ) 
9. h5 $1 Qd8 10. c3 { Preparing c3-c4, so that one pawn stops two. } bxc3 11. bxc3 Qc7 12. c4 $1 Qd8 13. Qc6 Qc7 ( { After } 13... Qxa5 $6 14. Qxd6 Kb7 15. Qe7 Kb6 ( { or } 15... Qc7 16. Qe4 ) 
16. Qxf7 Qe1 17. Kg2 Qe4 18. f3 Qe2 19. Kh3 { White has excellent winning chances. } ) 
14. Qe8+ Kb7 $2 { A tactical oversight. } ( { Black had to play } 14... Ka7 { though after } 15. Qe4 h6 ( { or } 15... g6 16. h6 $1 ) 
16. Kh2 $1 { preparing 17 Qg4 and a future invasion of the king along the light squares, White keeps a clear advantage. } ) 
15. Qe4+ ( { Black resigned, in view of } 15. Qe4 Ka7 16. Qxh7 Qxa5 17. Qxg7 Qe1 18. Kg2 Qe4 19. Kh3 { etc. } ) 

 1-0

