 { In positions where both sides have castled short, a pawn chain on f2-g3-h4 is often the most solid kingside formation once the endgame arrives. This is particularly true for the defender, who will only have one undefended pawn to worry about. As a result, a timely g2(g3)-g4 advance - or for Black, ...g7(g6)-g5 - can make a lot of sense, in order to gain space and prevent the opponent from achieving the aforementioned structure. We already saw a related case inAlekseev-Nepomniachtchi (in Chapter Four); here are some additional examples, featuring different types of endings and pawn structures. } ( { With White to move, } 1. h4 { , followed by Kg2 and the advance of the a-pawn, would be an excellent choice, with similar play to Example 216 (Gurevich-Glek) in Chapter Three. However it is Black's turn, and he takes appropriate action. } ) 
 { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Gaining space and neutralizing the plan outlined in the previous note. } 2. h3 ( 2. Kg2 g4 { was less productive. } ) 
Kg6 3. Ra6+ f6 4. a4 h5 $1 { Renewing the idea of ...g5-g4, cramping the enemy kingside. } 5. a5 ( { a) If } 5. Kf1 { then } g4 { anyway. } ) 
 ( { b) While } 5. g4 hxg4 ( { by the way, another good option is } 5. a5 h4 $5 { and 6...Ra3, targeting the h3-pawn; } ) 
6. hxg4 Kg7 { is also fine for Black - once the enemy king moves over to the opposite flank, the space gained by ...g7-g5 will prove useful in the kingside counterplay; e.g. } 7. Ra8 Kg6 8. a5 Kg7 9. a6 Kg6 10. Kg2 Kg7 11. Kf3 Kg6 12. Ke3 Kh7 13. f3 Ra3 14. Ke4 Kg7 15. Kd5 Rxf3 16. Rb8 ( { or } 16. Kc6 Ra3 17. Kb7 Rb3 18. Ka7 Rb4 { etc. } ) 
Ra3 17. Rb6 f5 $1 18. gxf5 g4 19. Kc6 Kf6 20. Kb7 Kxf5 { with a draw. } ) 
g4 $1 { With this and the next move, Black leaves White's king sidelined. } 6. hxg4 Ra1+ $1 7. Kg2 hxg4 8. Ra8 Kg7 9. a6 Kg6 10. f4 $5 Ra2+ 11. Kf1 Kf5 $1 12. a7 ( { After } 12. Ke1 $2 Ke4 $1 { it is Black who is playing for a win. } ) 
Ra1+ 13. Ke2 Ra2+ 14. Kd3 Ra3+ 15. Kd2 Ra2+ 16. Kc3 Ra3+ 17. Kb4 Ra1 18. Kc5 Ra3 19. Kb4 
 1/2-1/2

