 { White has a more active game, but Black is solid. Kacheishvili found a profound way of making progress. } 1. { -KEY- } Bxc6 $1 Rxc6 2. Rxc6 Bxc6 3. Nd4 Ke8 { Indirectly protecting the pawn. } ( 3... Bd7 $6 4. Rc7 { is less appealing. } ) 
4. Nxc6 Rc8 5. g4 $1 { The key move of the game - White hurries to gain space on the kingside, aware that only one weakness on c6 won't be enough to win the game. } ( { Less constructive was } 5. b4 $6 bxc6 6. b5 Kd7 ) 
bxc6 ( { After } 5... Rxc6 $6 6. Rxc6 bxc6 { the pawn ending is very pleasant for White, who could consider } 7. f4 $5 { gaining more space; e. g. } Kd7 8. Kd4 Kd6 9. e5 Ke6 10. b4 { with a long-term edge; } ( { but not } 9... bxc6 9. Kc5 $2 g5 $1 ) 
 ) 
6. Rc5 $1 { In Rubinstein style, White ties the enemy rook to the defence of the weak pawns before taking further action. } Kd7 7. Ra5 Rc7 8. b4 $1 Rb7 9. a3 Kc7 10. h4 { The time is right to continue the kingside expansion. } Kb6 { Black's play is logical too - the king takes care of the queenside pawns, so that the rook can be used for active means. } 11. h5 Rd7 12. f4 $1 { Gaining more space, and avoiding a target on the second rank. } Rd1 13. Re5 e6 14. h6 $1 { From now on, this pawn will be excellently placed in a potential race of passed pawns. } ( { Anyway } 14. f5 $5 { clearing some space for the rook also looked strong; e.g. } exf5 15. gxf5 $1 { preparing 16.Re7. } ) 
Ra1 ( { Or } 14... Rh1 15. g5 Rh3 16. Kd4 Rh4 17. Rc5 $1 Rxf4 18. Ke5 Rf3 19. Kd6 Kb7 20. Ke7 $1 { with the devastating threat of Kf8-g7. } ) 
15. Ra5 f6 { Preventing the entrance of the white king, but only temporarily. } 16. g5 $1 { Securing the h-pawn and softening up the dark squares for the king. Note the 'rook defends pawns' idea performed by the rook on a5. } fxg5 17. fxg5 a6 ( { After } 17... c5 18. Rxc5 Rxa3 19. Kf4 Rb3 20. Rc8 Rxb4 21. Rh8 { White is winning. } ) 
18. Kf4 Rg1 19. a4 ( { The immediate } 19. Re5 { was also strong, but first White advances his position on the queenside. } ) 
Rb1 20. Re5 Rf1+ ( { Or } 20... Rxb4 21. a5 Kc7 22. Rxe6 Kd7 23. Rf6 { with similar play. } ) 
21. Ke3 Rg1 22. a5+ Kc7 23. Rxe6 Kd7 24. Rf6 Rxg5 25. Rf7+ Ke6 26. Rxh7 { With Black helpless against the duo of the h- and e-pawns, the rest is easy. } Ke5 27. Re7+ Kf6 ( { After } 27... Kd6 28. Re8 Rh5 29. Rh8 Kc7 30. h7 Kb7 31. Kf4 { the e-pawn decides. } ) 
28. Rc7 Ke5 29. Rxc6 Rg3+ 30. Kf2 Rb3 31. Rxg6 Kxe4 32. Rg4+ $1 Kf5 33. Rh4 Rb2+ 34. Ke3 Rb3+ 35. Kd2 
 1-0

