 { Thanks to his active rook White is a bit better. Ivanchuk now found a multi-purpose move, one that many elite players would effect without much thinking, as a product of patternized memory, perhaps. } 1. { -KEY- } g4 $1 { Avoiding any first-rank mates, grabbing space, preventing the ideal enemy structure with pawns on f7-g6-h5, and clearing a path for the king. } h6 ( { Black has the same need to avoid a back-rank mate, and decides to put a pawn on the 'wrong' colour, since } 1... g6 2. g5 { would leave his kingside rather cramped. } ) 
2. Kg2 Bc3 3. g5 ( { Possibly } 3. Rb7 $1 { was more technical, securing the b-pawn while keeping the rook on the seventh rank; e.g. } g6 4. Rb6 Kg7 5. h4 $1 Rc7 6. Kg3 { keeping a slight edge - Ivanchuk. } ) 
Rb8 4. Rd3 Rc8 ( { After } 4... Rxb3 $6 5. gxh6 gxh6 6. Nd4 { the pin is very annoying for Black; e.g. } Ra3 7. Nc2 Rb3 8. e4 $1 Kg7 9. Ne3 Ra3 10. Nc4 Rb3 11. Nxa5 Ra3 12. Nc4 Rb3 13. Kf3 { preparing Ke2-d1-c2 - Krasenkow. } ) 
5. h4 hxg5 $6 ( 5... Kh7 6. Kg3 Kg6 { was preferable, so that } 7. Kg4 { can be met by } h5 $1 8. Kg3 f6 { - Ivanchuk, with White's king being less active than in the game. } ) 
6. hxg5 Kh7 7. Kg3 ( { Ivanchuk also suggests } 7. Rd7 Kg6 8. Rb7 f6 9. Rb6 $1 { in similar spirit to the 3 Rb7 subline above. } ) 
Kg6 8. Kf4 Rc6 9. e4 f6 10. Kg4 fxg5 $6 { Another dubious capture, which leaves Black's kingside broken and yields White more space in which to operate. } ( { More sound was } 10... Bb2 11. Nh4 Kf7 12. f4 { with just a slight edge for White - Ivanchuk. } ) 
11. Nxg5 e5 { Virtually forced, in order to prevent f2-f4-f5. } 12. Nf3 $1 { Some kind of zugzwang has emerged: rook moves along the c-file permit 13.Rd6+, while bishop moves to b2 or a1 run into 13.Rd5, winning a pawn. Thus Black is forced to allow White's king to invade, in pawn ending fashion. } Kf6 13. Kh5 $1 Ke7 ( { Active defence with } 13... Ke6 14. Kg6 Bd4 { fails to } 15. Nxd4 exd4 16. f4 $1 { - Ivanchuk. } ) 
14. Nh4 { The knight finally gets closer to the light square weaknesses in the enemy camp. } ( { Another option was } 14. Kg5 $5 { with a similar zugzwang as two moves ago; e.g. } Ke6 15. Kg6 $1 Rc7 16. Nh4 { followed by Nf5. } ) 
Kf6 15. Rf3+ Ke6 16. Nf5 Kd7 $6 ( { The cold-blooded } 16... Kf7 { was better, when } 17. Nd6 Ke7 18. Nc4 { keeps the pressure, but at least material equality remains intact. } ) 
17. Rd3+ Kc7 18. Nxg7 Be1 19. f3 { For a player of Ivanchuk's calibre, the rest is a matter of technique, though the coming exchange of rooks probably helped him a bit. } Rc3 $6 20. Ne6+ $1 { Creating a barrier for Black's king before swapping the rooks. } Kc6 21. Rxc3+ Bxc3 22. Kg6 Kd6 23. Kf5 Kc6 24. f4 exf4 25. Kxf4 { If the bishop was a knight and the knight a bishop, then Black would have reasonable hopes of a blockade, but here the white knight is too strong. } Bf6 ( { Or } 25... Kd6 26. Kf5 Ba1 27. Nf4 Kc5 28. Nd3 Kd4 29. e5 $1 { etc. } ) 
26. e5 Be7 27. Ke4 Bh4 28. Kd4 Bg3 29. Ng5 $1 Bf4 30. Nf3 Bg3 31. Kc4 { In this hopeless position, Black resigned. One further plan for White is Nd4+, Kd5 and Ne6-c5, winning the a6-pawn - Baburin. } 
