 { As we already discussed in Chapter Four, once there are bishops on the board, the colour of the squares on which the pawns of both sides are located becomes a key factor. Here the queenside structure is already in White's favour, but this fact alone won't be sufficient for victory, and Carlsen now turns his attention to the opposite flank. } 1. { -KEY- } g4 $1 { Gaining space, preventing ...h6-h5 with an ideal defensive structure for Black, and fixing the h6-pawn as a target. } ( { Of course } 1. Rxe8 $6 Rxe8 2. Bxb6 $4 { failed to } Rb8 ) 
Kf8 2. h4 Rxe4 ( { Here and later, } 2... h5 { is strongly met by } 3. gxh5 gxh5 4. Rb5 $1 ) 
3. Kxe4 Re8+ 4. Kd3 Re6 5. Be3 $1 Kg7 6. Rb5 { A useful preparation for the further expansion on the kingside. Deprived of any counterplay, Black can expect a long evening. } Bd8 7. h5 $1 Rd6+ 8. Kc4 Rc6+ ( { If } 8... f5 { then } 9. f3 $1 fxg4 10. fxg4 gxh5 11. gxh5 { and Black is still a long way from a draw - Carlsen. } ) 
9. Kd5 Re6 10. Bd4+ Kf8 11. f4 $1 { More space. In Carlsen's own words: 'With the pawn coming to f5, I felt pretty sure that I was winning. White just gains more and more ground, and eventually Black cannot hold on to everything.' } Bc7 12. f5 Rd6+ 13. Ke4 Rc6 14. Rb1 $1 { A timely shift of angle, in order to attack the h6-pawn. } Ke8 ( { a) Black could have prevented the further course of the game with the ugly } 14... g5 { but the invasion of White's king on the queenside and the permanent risk of Bd4-g7 would make his task difficult anyway; e.g. } 15. Kd5 Rd6 16. Kc4 Ke7 17. Kb5 Kd7 18. Ka6 Rc6 19. Kb7 Rc4 20. Bg7 $1 Bd6 21. Bxh6 Rxg4 22. Bg7 Rh4 23. Rd1 Ke7 24. Kc6 $1 Rc4 25. Kxb6 Rxa4 26. Rh1 { and the h-pawn decides - Carlsen. } ) 
 ( { b) Carlsen also indicates that } 14... gxh5 15. gxh5 Rc4 { so as to keep the h-file closed, would run into } 16. Ra1 Rc6 17. Rg1 Rd6 ( { or } 17... Rc4 18. Kd5 Rxa4 19. Rg4 $1 Ra1 20. Kc6 Bh2 21. Bg7 Ke8 22. Re4 Kd8 23. Bf6 { etc. } ) 
18. Be5 $1 Rc6 19. Bg7 Ke7 20. Bd4 Kf8 21. Rg7 { and the rook invades with decisive effect. } ) 
15. hxg6 fxg6 16. Rh1 Kf7 $6 ( 16... gxf5 17. gxf5 Bd8 { was more tenacious, although after } 18. f6 $1 Bxf6 19. Rxh6 Rxc3 $1 20. Bxf6 $1 { the ensuing rook and bishop vs. rook ending is won due to Black's cut-off king and White's well-placed king and bishop; e.g. } Ra3 ( { or } 20... Rc4 21. Kd5 Rxa4 22. Ke6 Re4 23. Be5 ) 
21. Rh7 $1 Rxa4 22. Kd5 Ra2 23. Ke6 Re2 24. Be5 Rf2 25. Rb7 Rd2 26. Rxb6 { - Carlsen. } ) 
17. Kd5 { Just like in the 14...g5 subline above, White directs his king to the opposite flank, while enabling the threat of 18.Rxh6. } Rd6+ 18. Kc4 gxf5 ( { After } 18... g5 19. Kb5 Bd8 20. Ka6 Rc6 21. Rh3 $1 ( { a first zugzwang, avoiding } 20... gxf5 20. Kb7 Rc7 ) 
Rd6 ( { or } 21... Kg8 22. f6 $1 ) 
22. Kb7 $1 Ke8 23. Rh1 Kf7 24. Kc8 { a second zugzwang puts an end to Black's defence - Carlsen. } ) 
19. gxf5 Bd8 20. f6 $1 { By now this advance is of no surprise to us. Here it helps White to swap the rooks, after which Black's queenside pawns are easy prey. } Bxf6 ( { Or } 20... Kg6 21. Rg1 Kf7 22. Rg7 Ke8 23. Rg6 Kf7 24. Rxh6 { etc - Carlsen. } ) 
21. Rxh6 Be7 22. Rxd6 Bxd6 23. Kb5 Ke6 24. Bxb6 Kd7 25. c4 Kc8 26. Bxa5 ( { White must of course avoid } 26. c5 $2 Bxc5 $1 { with a theoretical draw. In the following, he realizes his advantage by means of bridges with the bishop. Interestingly, Carlsen mentions that he already knew of the winning method from the game R.J.Fischer-P.Keres, Zürich 1959, where an identical ending was reached on the opposite flank. } ) 
Kb7 27. Bb4 $1 Bf4 28. c5 Ka7 29. c6 Kb8 30. a5 Ka7 31. a6 Ka8 32. Bc5 $1 { White should transfer his king to d7 in order to create a new bridge on the a5-d8 diagonal, but first he must prevent ...Ka7. } Bb8 33. Kc4 Bc7 34. Kd5 Bd8 35. Ke6 Bc7 36. Kd7 Ba5 37. Be7 $1 ( { In view of } 38. Bd8 { next, Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

