 { The isolated d4-pawn implies a slight but enduring edge for Black in this endgame. On the other hand, as I think Baburin mentioned on some occasion, knights often prove more useful than bishops in the IQP structure. Before taking any other action, Dreev hurries to make a useful move on the kingside. } 1... { -KEY- } g5 $1 { Thus Black gains some space and prevents a desirable h3-h4. Moreover, the fact that the enemy kingside pawns are temporarily fixed on the same colour as his bishop favours him too. } 2. g3 Bb5 3. Nd1 { Pelletier starts improving his knights. } ( { In contrast, after } 3. h4 $6 gxh4 4. gxh4 { his f- and h-pawns would just become exposed to the enemy pieces. } ) 
Ne8 $1 4. Nc3 Bc6 5. Nd3 Nd6 { A good post for the knight, from where it has further interesting destinies such as b5, c4 and f5. } 6. f4 { Getting rid of a weakness, at the cost of somewhat opening the position for the bishop. } ( { As for the advance of the IQP by } 6. Nb4 Bb7 7. d5 { Black keeps the advantage after the simple } exd5 8. Nbxd5 Ke6 ) 
h6 $1 { Maintaining the restrictive pawn on g5. } 7. g4 { Denying the black knight the f5-square, but also fixing pawns on the 'wrong' colour. } ( { Then again, it is hard to suggest anything better; e. g. the active } 7. fxg5 $6 hxg5 8. h4 { backfires after } gxh4 9. gxh4 Nf5 { - Lukacs. } ) 
Bb7 $1 8. Kg3 Nc4 { Thanks to his previous, prophylactic move, Black does not have to worry about 9.Ne5. } 9. h4 f6 { In the same spirit as three moves ago. } ( 9... gxh4 $5 10. Kxh4 Ne3 { was also interesting, intending ...Nc2 at the right moment. } ) 
10. hxg5 hxg5 11. fxg5 fxg5 12. Kf2 a6 13. a4 $6 ( { Pelletier aims to chase away the knight by b2-b3, but now more pawns are fixed on squares of the 'wrong' colour - Lukacs; who suggests } 13. Nd1 { instead. } ) 
Kd6 14. b3 Nd2 15. Nc1 $6 ( { In view of Black's strong reply, active defence with } 15. Ne5 Nxb3 16. Ke3 { was a lesser evil - Lukacs. } ) 
 ( { Even the simple } 15. b4 { was much better than the text, for example } Ne4 16. Nxe4 Bxe4 17. Ne5 $1 Kd5 18. Ke3 { with sufficient counterplay. } ) 
e5 $1 { Usually pawn exchanges favour the defender, but here Black is correct in clearing some space for his king. We saw a similar case in Example 257 (Van Wely-Kramnik) in Chapter Four. } 16. dxe5+ ( { Or } 16. Ke3 Nf1 { heading for h2, just like in the game. } ) 
Kxe5 17. Ke3 Nf1+ 18. Kf2 Nh2 19. Kg3 Nf3 20. N1e2 { Undefending the b3-pawn, but otherwise 20...Kd4 was a threat. } Nd2 $1 21. b4 Bc8 { With weaknesses on both flanks and Black's king dominating the board, the rest is a nightmare for White. } 22. b5 a5 $1 { Avoiding any further pawn exchanges, while fixing a target on a4. } 23. Ng1 ( { White was short of solid moves; e.g. } 23. Nd1 Be6 24. Nb2 Ke4 { and the black king enters anyway. } ) 
Kd4 $1 24. Nce2+ Kd3 25. Nc1+ Ke3 26. Nce2 Ne4+ ( { If } 26... Ne4 27. Kh3 { then } Kf2 $1 { is one of several good replies, completely paralysing the enemy forces. } ) 

 0-1

