[FEN "2r2rk1/pp3npp/4p3/n2pPp2/3P1B2/5PP1/PP2BK1P/2RR4 b - - 0 1"]

 { Again White enjoys more space and, despite the closed centre, his bishop pair can be considered superior to Black's knights, which don't have a stronghold on any part on the board. } 1... { -KEY- } Rxc1 2. Bxc1 $1 { A surprising recapture, but Spassky wants to defend the vulnerable d4-pawn with his king, so that the rook and dark-squared bishop can take upon more active tasks. } Rc8 3. Bd2 Nc6 ( { An alternative was } 3... Nc4 4. Rc1 Nb6 $1 5. Rxc8 Nxc8 { in order to swap the white rook, which will play an important role in the further course of the game. } ) 
4. Ke3 Kf8 5. b4 $1 { In Curt Hansen's words, White aims to advance his queenside pawns to a5 and b5 in order to deprive Black's knights of the c6- and b6-squares, and indirectly limit any counterplay on that part of the board. } Nb8 ( { a) After } 5... a6 $6 6. a4 { followed by b4-b5, Black would have an additional problem with the defence of the a-file. Note, too, that Spassky is never interested in swapping rooks with Rc1, as this piece will be useful for him as soon as a new file is opened on any of the flanks; besides, his bishop pair has an easy task controlling the entry squares on c3 and c2. } ) 
 ( { b) As for the unexpected } 5... f4 $5 { White would reply } 6. Kd3 $1 { when the opening of files on the kingside is in his favour; } ( { rather than } 5... Nb8 5. gxf4 Nh6 { followed by ...Nf5. } ) 
 ) 
6. a4 Nd7 7. a5 $1 { Of course this pawn goes first, in order to prevent ...Nb6-c4. } Nd8 ( { This makes little sense after White's reply; but Black was rather short of active options, with } 7... Rc2 8. Bd3 Rb2 $2 { backfiring after } 9. Rc1 ) 
8. b5 Nf7 9. Bd3 g5 ( { Thus Black prepares to meet } 9... g5 10. g4 { with } f4 { followed by ...Kg7 and ...Nf8-g6, but White finds a more efficient way of softening up the enemy kingside. } ) 
10. h4 $1 h6 11. Rh1 Kg7 12. Ke2 $1 { Spassky assures that once he is ready for g3-g4, the possible reply ...f5-f4 won't come with check. } Nf8 13. Bb4 $1 { Ever since 8.b5 this move has been in the air, but only now White executes it, when the threat 14.Bc5 practically forces the return of the knight to d7. } Nd7 14. Kd2 Rc7 15. hxg5 Nxg5 ( { This leads to a bad structure; but opening the h-file with } 15... hxg5 { was risky in view of } 16. g4 $1 { e.g. } fxg4 17. fxg4 Kg8 18. Bd6 $1 ( 17... Nxg5 17. Rh7 { with zugzwang motifs, is interesting too; } ) 
Rc8 ( { or } 18... Nxd6 19. exd6 Rc8 20. Rh7 ) 
19. Be7 Re8 20. Bf6 Nxf6 21. exf6 e5 22. Bh7 Kf8 23. Bg6 { with a decisive 24.Rh7 next - Hansen. } ) 
16. Ke2 Rc8 17. Bd2 { White's plan now is to break with g3-g4 at the most suitable moment. } Nf8 18. Rg1 Ng6 19. Rb1 ( { No hurry; though } 19. g4 $5 { was interesting too; e.g. } Nh3 20. Rg3 $1 Nhf4 21. Bxf4 Nxf4 22. Ke3 Nxd3 23. gxf5 Kf8 24. Kxd3 exf5 25. Rg6 { with a horrible rook ending for Black. } ) 
Nf8 20. Bb4 Nd7 21. Ke3 Kg8 22. Rh1 Kg7 23. Kf4 Nf8 { How to progress? Well, as we already saw inExample 276 (Hamdouchi-Bricard) in Chapter Four, one of the virtues of the bishop pair is the possibility to exchange one of them in return for a different advantage. } ( { a) After } 23... Rc7 24. g4 fxg4 25. Kxg4 $1 { with ideas like Bd2 and f4-f5, Black is again in trouble. } ) 
 ( { b) With } 23... Nf8 { Cramling prepares to meet } 24. g4 { with } Ng6 { but runs into a new problem. } ) 
24. Bxf8+ $1 Rxf8 ( { From now on Black will suffer heavily along the c-file; but } 24... Kxf8 $2 { obviously failed to } 25. Rxh6 ) 
25. Rc1 b6 { Cramling avoids an unpleasant b5-b6, with ideas like Rc7 and B(x)a6. } 26. a6 $1 ( { Fixing a target on a7; while avoiding even the slightest glimpse of counterplay after } 26. axb6 axb6 27. Rc6 Ra8 ) 
Rf7 27. Ke3 $1 h5 ( { This pawn soon becomes a new weakness; but after } 27... Nh7 28. Rc8 Nf8 29. f4 { Black's task was difficult anyway; e.g. } Re7 ( { or } 29... Kg6 30. g4 ) 
30. Be2 $1 Rf7 31. Bh5 Re7 32. Rb8 Kg8 ( { if } 32... Rd7 { then } 33. Be8 Re7 34. Rb7 $1 ) 
33. g4 fxg4 34. Bxg4 { with a decisive zugzwang. } ) 
28. Rc8 Nh7 ( { Or } 28... Rf8 29. Rc6 { with the threat of f3-f4 - Hansen. } ) 
29. f4 Nf8 30. Be2 Kh6 31. Re8 Kg6 32. Kf2 $1 { The king's march to h4 will finally break down Black's defences. } Kh6 33. Bf3 Kg6 34. Kg2 h4 ( 34... Kh6 35. Kh3 Kg6 36. Kh4 { is equally hopeless. } ) 
35. gxh4 Kg7 36. h5 Kh6 37. Kh3 Kg7 38. Kh4 Kh6 39. Bxd5 $1 ( { In view of } 39. Bxd5 exd5 40. e6 Rf6 41. e7 { Black resigned. } ) 

 1-0

